ut just  the way it was written about in terms of  what was done the bomb being dropped it  disgruntled American veterans it made  America look and this by the way this  exhibit didn't actually end up being  this way there was so much controversy  about how it was being presented  American veterans of World War two hated  the thought that Americans would be  portrayed as the evil people in this  conflict I mean in any war evil things  are done on both sides and and people  felt that we owed no apologies to Japan  but they owed apologies to us that was  some of the stuff that was being talked  about it it was good I think that we all  had to think about this that was the  important talking that was being done  during that 50th anniversary in your  book you discuss a lot of what's going  on at Los Alamos today how has Los  Alamos changed from the Cold War period  what were we trying to do at Los Alamos  during the Cold War from 1945 on and now  in the post-cold war world what are they  doing at Los Alamos well during that  Cold War period of course the laboratory  was working on nuclear weapons refining  their art making better and more  efficient weapons in serving their  client the Department of Defense they  were also all that time working on other  science and that's something that people  don't always know about and since the  end of the Cold War they have tried to  find another focus for their technology  however nuclear weapons are still very  important we have a lot of them in our  stockpile and their concern is being a  good steward of those weapons so that  God forbid if they should ever have to  be used they work properly so that the  military has confidence in them and so  that  the natural degradation of materials in  nuclear weapons is understood so that  people can understand how they might  perform that's one whole important area  of research at the laboratory that has  also incidentally stabilized their  budgetary problems after the end of the  Cold War there was a state of flux for  the laboratory not really understanding  what was going to happen and how they  would be involved in national life he  mentioned the Department of Defense  explained to us what department oversees  Los Alamos where do they get their  funding how does that work the  Department of Energy is the overseer for  Los Alamos National Laboratory and all  the other laboratories and the money  comes from the American taxpayer they're  funded by Congress now how did you  research this book did you research it  in Washington did you live in Los Alamos  and what papers did you read and who did  you interview I spend a lot of time in  Los Alamos and I should explain that  this book was meant to be and it's what  I think I ended up with a portrait of  the community and of the laboratory and  portraits of the people who live and  work there and so the most important  part of my research was being there I  spent a lot of time there and I did over  100 formal interviews and many more  informal interviews  and read probably every book that's  available about the history of the  laboratory and well it was a tremendous  research project I can't begin to tell  you how many megabytes of computer disks  of information I have not to mention the  stack of books of transcribed interviews  I have so it's a big project when did  you begin your research and how long did  the book take you to write i started in  1992 started really by visiting their  the first time and it took three or four  years to finish the project where do you  live normally I live in Minnesota which  and that's the first question i usually  get from interviewers is why would  someone from some 1,400 miles away  decide to do a book on a laboratory in  New Mexico and you know first of all I  think there is real merit in accounts  written by people who aren't too close  who don't have a lot of assumptions  lifetime assumptions possibly if you're  from New Mexico about an institution and  beyond that I came here working on a new  story and was immediately fascinated  with the place and wanted to know more  and eventually it became a book tell us  about your background is this your first  book and it sounds like you're a  reporter what is your background um I my  in my past I have been a reporter  science and medical reporter i have  written for newspapers and magazines  and peer reviewed journals and for  science museums and I have a very  background that prepared me to do  something like this where did you go to  college I went to the College of st.  Catherine in st. Paul Minnesota  what did you study there I'm actually  I'm a literature major and then I went  back to school to take science courses  because I decided I wanted to be a  science reporter why well I have read a  lot of good science writers and I think  it's a good thing to do I think it's  needed I enjoy being around research  scientists and doctors and I like the  process of translating difficult  information so that regular people can  understand it and I also think that's  very important where our lives are just  entangled in technology and we benefit  and suffer from technology and I think  everyday folks ought to know what it's  all about because we have to make  decisions tell us about why you called  the book secret Mesa and did you go into  Los Alamos lab yourself and what did you  see well of course I went into the  laboratory and I did go behind the fence  which is what they call secure areas and  I was escorted of course and i called it  secret mesa because it's on this  isolated high plateau on a mesa and it  began as a very secret obviously a very  secret installation and it was closed he  was still a secret installation and a  secret town until the 50s you just  couldn't go visit there it was they have  gates and one of the towers one of the  guard towers is still there on the main  road into town and you had to go through  jump through a few hoops to get into  town so then I think that the beginnings  of the town the Cold War habits of  secrecy the necessity for secrecy still  resonate there it is still a community  that is trying to figure out how to be  more open how to be  giving of information to the public I  think now they want the public to  understand them and appreciate them and  not make knee-jerk responses to their  research which is not just weapons  research that's really important they  feel as though they're painted by a very  broad brush there's research there into  the human genome there's an AIDS  database there that is serving  researchers in aids all over the world  there's so much going on their material  science research that affects our cars  our pop cans you know it affects a lot  of things tell us if you're an average  citizen not a reporter a writer but if  you wanted to go and visit Los Alamos  could you look around or how could you  learn more about Los Alamos well of  course you can go there the first time I  went there I had no clearance I had no  appointments I just walked into town  it's a it's a town with normal  neighborhoods and the laboratory is by  and large fairly open they do like  people to check in with their visitors  center and I didn't do that and as a  result some goofy things happened I was  driving around on the roads up there and  I just stop and take a picture of what i  thought was the Santa Fe National Forest  well it was but it was much more than  that it was laboratory property and  immediately a little runabout vehicles  did a u-turn in the middle of the  highway came and sat beside me until I  drove away didn't say anything to me  just watched and then another time it  happened and then I began to realize  this wasn't just the Santa Fe National  Forest there was something else going on  there and I came to understand then what  that was all about they have a security  force they watch people very carefully  there's more plutonium at  los alamos than anywhere in this country  did you ever see plutonium when you were  there actually I didn't I begged and  pleaded to be allowed to go into ta 55  their plutonium research facility and  then after I finished with the book they  did take reporters through and I've been  promised that next time they do it I get  to go and I really would like to in your  book you you've obviously interests  interviewed a great number of scientists  at the laboratory tell us about some of  the more unusual people in town that you  may have spoken with well townfolk or a  research folk because there are unusual  people and both department either one  whichever you think might be of interest  to the story well I really enjoyed  talking to the weapons folk of course  because they are very interesting and  shed light on very scary and interesting  topics like nuclear terrorism and I  talked to a robotics expert who is just  a crazy man a wild man inventive guy  who's making robots that are independent  he puts them down and lets them figure  out the environment they're in little  robotic ants and other weird things did  you speak to any people who are  anti-nuclear or against the nuclear  plant well of course and there's one  fellow in town he's notorious his name  is it Edward growth us and he refers to  himself as Eduardo de los alamos and  he's very colorful he has a salvage  business in town that is full of old lab  stuff basically he has bought up their  garbage the stuff they don't want  anymore some of it very valuable and and  still usable by people who need  scientific equipment and can't afford  the most  and he has he's a peace activist this is  this is that him this is him in front of  what he refers to was the black hole  which is the place where he keeps all of  his stuff and he is interestingly a  former lab employee and he's lived in  the town since nineteen forty nine and  he stays there even though he hates the  business of the laboratory because as he  said all of my friends are there all of  my enemies are there where else would I  go and he's tolerated which is an  interesting comment on the community I  think they did they're big boys and  girls with big shoulders and they can  take a lot of criticism including from  Eduardo who is kind of like that  eccentric uncle who's kind of a pain in  the ass but everybody puts up with him  because he's part of the family and this  is a very interesting guy you've brought  something with you here today it looks  like a can can you tell us what it is  and the story behind it well this is a  can says organic plutonium and has a  mushroom cloud on it and it's from the  reactors of Los Alamos New Mexico the  home of the atomic bomb well of course  it's not really plutonium that would  that would not be good but it is  something that has I think it's peas or  corn but it's been relabeled by eduardo  de los alamos and he sells it in his  store you know it's like a 79 kid said  canapés that he's selling for 512 it was  I think but the interesting thing is he  sent one of these to President Clinton  nobody laughed I don't think it was  considered a funny thing and a couple of  Secret Service agents showed up at his  door and questioned him thoroughly hours  I think and in the end realized it was  just a publicity stunt and nothing  harmful and and mr. growth has said they  were actually very nice peaceful can I  kind of made friends with them so this  is kind of thing that he does it's fun  as a reporter how do you find someone  like mr. growth asst are you in the town  looking for that kind of character did  you read about him how'd you find that  kind of source it wasn't hard to find  Edgar oh--this he found finds everybody  else there are a lot of articles written  about him I read something in Scientific  American I think he's had television  appearances and all kinds of things he  was the least difficult person to find  there are other people that you have to  be very convincing to get them to talk  to you and when just in terms of the  townspeople I had one woman say to me  what kind of book are you writing I mean  she was really cautious she said we've  been burned so many times you won't get  anybody to talk to you if you don't  prove to them somehow for by force of  will if nothing else that you're  planning to write a balanced piece and  so I was lucky enough that I did that  and got a lot of candid remarks from  people as an author how do you talk to  your sources and convince them that you  are trying to write a balanced account  very carefully very carefully I don't  know you know I don't even know if I can  explain what that is it's something that  you learned to do connect with people on  some level and make them feel that they  can trust you that you're not going to  do wrong by them that you're going to  honor in some way what they do and I  think it's different in a  confrontational interview and an  investigative reporting I wasn't doing  that and also in Los Alamos this is  critically important you have to know  how to speak their language if you if  you don't know the words if you don't  understand at least the most rudimentary  aspects of their technology the window  blinds go down pretty quickly and you  can see it happen and you know you're in  trouble so preparation is an important  part of it  what sticks out in your mind the most is  something that surprised you about Los  Alamos that you found out in the course  of writing this book what was the  biggest surprise to you if there was one  well the biggest surprise just in terms  of you know function in the process of  doing the book was how eager people were  to talk and tell their story and how  much they could tell they weren't as  secretive as I thought they'd be I mean  there's a lot of that still and there  are a lot of things that they're very  careful not to talk about they're good  at it but I also in terms of the things  i learned i was surprised at how  involved the laboratory is encounter  proliferation how involved they are in  intelligence in terms of what's going on  now the proliferation of weapons of mass  destruction and i heard stories from  them that just scared the willies out of  me i mean it's it's scary stuff this  terrorism business so would you say  these stories were about Russians or are  the scary stories more about other  countries these days well the scary  stories at least what scared me was the  ease with which people with not good  intentions may be able to get their  hands on materials that can cause a big  mess you know either by contamination  with radioactive products or god forbid  carry off a weapon and we saw in the  news not long ago that Russian that  Russia could not account for a large  number of suitcase size nuclear weapons  and that was something that they had  talked about it was Talan  when I was visiting their years you know  several years ago the potential for that  there are weapons that are very  destructive and very small and as one  guy told me he said you could drive one  inch of New York in an old Corvair you  know that's what that's what frightened  me most of all tell us a little bit  about Los Alamos and public opinion in  your book you talk a little bit about  the radiation experiments and how that  information was released to the public  how do you think that is still unfolding  today with the radiation experiments and  when were they well in terms of the  radiation experiments we do sees stories  once in a while but I think that was the  blip on the public consciousness that  now that's over there's other stuff I  suppose to think about but it's part of  a continuing problem i think that the  public doesn't trust weapons scientists  surprise i mean i don't think they trust  government scientists people don't trust  the government frankly and so it's  difficult for people in our institutions  our scientific institutions or  governmental institutions to to get  people to really trust them and for the  laboratory this is going to be a problem  for a while I think just in terms of  talking about materials or or their work  I mean everyone is always saying they're  they're designing new weapons aren't  they they're not supposed to be of  course well no maybe they are i don't  know but they're never going to convince  the public that they aren't who do you  hope reads this book secret mesa when  you were writing it did you have a  certain reader in mind well i think it  could be interesting to a lot of people  anyone who grew up during the Cold War  would certainly be able to hook into  this because we all grew up with that  terror that thought that it could all be  over and to know where a lot of the  technology comes from could be  interesting for people who lived through  World War two and understood at the end  what brought it to a conclusion it might  be interesting to them it's written so  that it's accessible to anyone who has  any interest in technology in government  science in history and also in good  stories I mean there's some really  interesting people in that town and I  got to talk to quite a few of them so if  nothing else it's I think it's a good  read have you received any criticism  from anyone on on any side of the  nuclear industry over this book not not  from people in the industry no one  gentleman last night wished that I had  written more about the other science at  the laboratory and I do too but I really  think I wrote about the things that are  most worrisome to everyday Americans so  I had to focus and that's what I did  maybe there's another book in all of  that I don't know that was exactly what  I was thinking now that you've spent so  many years on this book and it's  published and you're out talking about  it have you had a chance to think about  your next book and what might that be  about well I'm not sure I'm not sure yet  I'm very interested in the idea of  storing nuclear or chemical wastes on  Indian lands and so I've been doing some  research about that I don't have a book  book contract in that  that yet I would like to do more about  los alamos national laboratory or about  the wider community we'll just have to  see there's so much that can be written  it's it's a pretty deep well how do you  think this book will sell and who's your  publisher by the way the publisher is  john wiley &amp; sons and i don't know how  it will sell i mean that's that's a good  question you never know and then this is  what I hear from my editor and from  people in my publisher you just don't  know how to call it sometimes what will  interest people what will pique their  interest it's it's a it's a little magic  well we thank you Joe and schreier for  spending some time with us to talk about  your new book secret Mesa inside los  alamos national laboratory thank you
 

